{"schema":"zzx-html-edition-profile-v1","country":"VI","name":"Virgin Islands","year":2007,"status":"source-extracted; field review pending","source":{"name":"factbook-2007.zip","member":"factbook-2007/geos/vq.html","sha256":"c24b662116fe0d7a4efbd328566dbded83e525be77238327134d03b9f27d0eb1","format":"html"},"rights_notice_member":"factbook-2007/docs/contributor_copyright.html","rights_notice_sha256":"0aa0c111c3015a738f223e5be4ed3275a829514a52d1781742a8671e915bdb1e","fields":[{"category":"introduction","label":"Background","content":"During the 17th century, the archipelago was divided into two territorial units, one English and the other Danish. Sugarcane, produced by slave labor, drove the islands' economy during the 18th and early 19th centuries. In 1917, the US purchased the Danish portion, which had been in economic decline since the abolition of slavery in 1848.","ordinal":1,"locator":"factbook-2007/geos/vq.html#field:1","country":"VI","edition_year":2007},{"category":"geography","label":"Location","content":"Caribbean, islands between the Caribbean Sea and the North Atlantic Ocean, east of Puerto Rico","ordinal":2,"locator":"factbook-2007/geos/vq.html#field:2","country":"VI","edition_year":2007},{"category":"geography","label":"Geographic coordinates","content":"18 20 N, 64 50 W","ordinal":3,"locator":"factbook-2007/geos/vq.html#field:3","country":"VI","edition_year":2007},{"category":"geography","label":"Map references","content":"Central America and the Caribbean","ordinal":4,"locator":"factbook-2007/geos/vq.html#field:4","country":"VI","edition_year":2007},{"category":"geography","label":"Area","content":"total: 1,910 sq km\n\nland: 346 sq km\n\nwater: 1,564 sq km","ordinal":5,"locator":"factbook-2007/geos/vq.html#field:5","country":"VI","edition_year":2007},{"category":"geography","label":"Area - comparative","content":"twice the size of Washington, DC","ordinal":6,"locator":"factbook-2007/geos/vq.html#field:6","country":"VI","edition_year":2007},{"categor